ifferent but  if I were to select my profile and load  everything up I wouldn't lose any of my  friends I wouldn't lose my network or my  content or anything and even though this  change I made was kind of trivial you  can imagine being in a situation where  on social media where a change you'd  like to make is not trivial maybe you'd  like to have some say over how the color  scheme works because you are colorblind  or maybe you want better moderation  tools you're empowered to do that with  this model and I think that's just  fantastic now I'm not going to make any  promises that this type of model a  peer-to-peer web is going to fix  everything that's wrong with the web and  I'm not gonna promise that an app like  fritter is going to ever become as  popular as Facebook or Twitter because  they're already pretty darn popular but  I can promise that a peer-to-peer web  does make apps like this possible and I  can say that with confidence because I  use fritter every single day and so do a  lot of my friends if you want to learn  more about beaker um please just talk to  me afterwards I'm really excited to  share more about it or you can visit any  of these links to learn more and I want  to just end with this and say that I  think fundamentally a peer-to-peer web  is a more fun web that no matter what  your technical expertise is or your  access to resources it's a web you can  contribute you can build and you can  share and I think that makes it a  worthwhile experiment to run not just in  beaker but in every single browser thank  you so much for imagining with me
 

